From eb1bd8883f69ffec2d22f9ff050588d5e6ba525b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:34:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/31] chore(db): database tooling that works with and without
 Docker
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`supabase init` plus the scripts the phase needs to answer one question:
do the migrations rebuild the database from scratch?

`supabase start` is the official path and stays that way. But no Docker
daemon means no answer at all, so `scripts/db/local-postgres.sh` brings up
a throwaway Postgres cluster and `auth-shim.sql` recreates the minimum
`auth` surface the migrations depend on. `dev-db.sh` picks the engine and
says out loud which one it used — an environment that thinks it is running
Supabase while running something else draws wrong conclusions, and wrong
conclusions about a database are the expensive kind.

The shim mirrors two things on purpose, because getting them wrong would
make local tests lie:

  - `service_role` is created with BYPASSRLS, as in Supabase. That is what
    stops a test from "proving" isolation with the role that ignores it.
  - default privileges grant everything on `public` to anon and
    authenticated, as in Supabase. Without that the revoke in migration
    140008 would have nothing to revoke, and "anon has no privileges"
    would pass vacuously.

`fingerprint.sql` hashes columns, constraints, indexes, triggers, enums,
`app` functions and RLS flags, so two databases can be compared without
`supabase db diff` — and therefore without Docker.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GNCydr15SRkdUC4T2eACE5
