From 5de9b47d9f9885f0860fad9e58feb52192697079 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:34:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/31] feat(db): Milestone 1 schema as forward-only migrations
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

19 tables, 16 enums, five infrastructure functions in the `app` schema.

The rule the whole design rests on: `client_id` never comes from input.
`app.derive_client_id()` reads it from the parent row and overwrites
whatever the caller sent — not validating it, discarding it. Writing a
row of Client A stamped as Client B stops being possible even with a bug
in the application. It is `security definer` for a real reason: once RLS
lands in phase 4, the inserting user may not be able to see the parent
row, and without definer the derivation would fail precisely under RLS.

Alongside it, `app.enforce_immutable_columns()` freezes tenant after
insert — the classic RLS hole is an UPDATE policy that lets a row change
tenant — and `app.reject_mutation()` makes `activity_log` append-only for
everyone, including `service_role`, which RLS cannot reach.

Approval belongs to the version, never to the item (ADR-0007), so `hook`,
`caption` and `cta` live on `content_versions`: editing a caption after
approval cannot destroy what was approved. Partial unique indexes make the
double tap on "Approve" idempotent while still allowing a second request
for changes.

The last migration enables RLS on all 19 tables and creates no policy.
In Postgres that denies everything, which is the safe baseline for a
database that has no authorization written yet — it is not the same thing
as being secure for multi-tenancy. The policies, the `app.has_client_access()`
family and the isolation suite are phase 4. The migration says so in its
header, at length, because "RLS enabled on 19/19 tables" is exactly the
kind of line that gets quoted out of context.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
