From 5f927b7101243c7a8b056952ac7cad6191380e4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:34:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/31] fix(db): act on the Supabase linter findings from
 staging
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two corrections, as new migrations. The earlier ones had already been
applied to staging, and rewriting migration history to fix a WARN trades a
small problem for a large one.

`citext` moves out of `public`. An extension installed there publishes its
functions on the API surface — a dozen comparison functions nobody needs
exposed. It now lives in `extensions`, which already has `usage` for the
API roles on hosted Supabase and which the migration creates locally. The
grant is not cosmetic: schema usage is a prerequisite for using the type,
so without it a `select email from profiles` in phase 4 would fail because
of the column type rather than the policy — an error that costs an
afternoon to read correctly.

Three tables get the `client_id` index they were missing. The linter flags
21 uncovered foreign keys and most of them are noise at this stage:
`created_by`, `decided_by`, `author_id` and `submitted_by` only matter when
deleting a profile, and profiles here are disabled, not deleted. But
`content_comments`, `content_approvals` and `strategy_approvals` store
`client_id` without an index while their siblings have one, and phase 4
will filter every read by exactly that column. An RLS policy is a real read
path — arguably the most real one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GNCydr15SRkdUC4T2eACE5
---
 ...260901160001_move_citext_to_extensions.sql | 35 +++++++++++++++++++
 ...0901160002_index_tenant_on_leaf_tables.sql | 21 +++++++++++
 2 files changed, 56 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 supabase/migrations/20260901160001_move_citext_to_extensions.sql
 create mode 100644 supabase/migrations/20260901160002_index_tenant_on_leaf_tables.sql

diff --git a/supabase/migrations/20260901160001_move_citext_to_extensions.sql b/supabase/migrations/20260901160001_move_citext_to_extensions.sql
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4b83592
--- /dev/null
+++ b/supabase/migrations/20260901160001_move_citext_to_extensions.sql
@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+-- citext sai de `public` e vai para `extensions`
+-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+--
+-- Achado do linter do Supabase depois de aplicar a FASE 2 no staging:
+-- `extension_in_public` (WARN). Extensão instalada em `public` publica as
+-- funções dela na superfície da API — no caso do citext, uma dúzia de funções
+-- de comparação que ninguém precisa expor. A convenção do Supabase é manter
+-- extensão no schema `extensions`, que já existe lá e já tem `usage` para os
+-- papéis da API.
+--
+-- Por que migration nova em vez de corrigir a 20260901140001: migrations aqui
+-- são forward-only e a 140001 já foi aplicada no staging
+-- (.claude/rules/database.md). Reescrever história de migration para arrumar
+-- um WARN é trocar um problema pequeno por um grande.
+--
+-- O tipo se move junto com as colunas que o usam: `profiles.email`,
+-- `clients.slug`, `onboarding_templates.key` e `notifications.recipient_email`
+-- continuam citext, agora qualificado como `extensions.citext`.
+--
+-- DAQUI PARA A FRENTE: coluna citext nova se escreve `extensions.citext`.
+
+-- No Supabase hospedado este schema já existe. Localmente, sem Docker, não —
+-- e a migration precisa rodar nos dois.
+create schema if not exists extensions;
+
+-- `usage` no schema é pré-requisito para USAR O TIPO, não para ler tabela.
+-- Sem isto, na FASE 4 um `select email from profiles` falharia por causa do
+-- tipo da coluna, e não por causa da policy — erro que custaria uma tarde.
+grant usage on schema extensions to postgres, anon, authenticated, service_role;
+
+alter extension citext set schema extensions;
+
+comment on schema extensions is
+  'Extensoes. Fora de public para nao expor funcao de extensao na API.';
diff --git a/supabase/migrations/20260901160002_index_tenant_on_leaf_tables.sql b/supabase/migrations/20260901160002_index_tenant_on_leaf_tables.sql
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..562af81
--- /dev/null
+++ b/supabase/migrations/20260901160002_index_tenant_on_leaf_tables.sql
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+-- Índice de `client_id` nas três tabelas que ficaram sem ele
+-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+--
+-- O linter de performance do Supabase apontou 21 chaves estrangeiras sem
+-- índice de cobertura. A maioria é ruído nesta fase: `created_by`,
+-- `decided_by`, `author_id`, `submitted_by` só pesam quando se APAGA um perfil,
+-- e perfil aqui não é apagado — é desabilitado. Criar oito índices para um
+-- caminho que não roda é exatamente o excesso que docs/spec-review.md §4 manda
+-- evitar.
+--
+-- Três, porém, não são ruído. `content_comments`, `content_approvals` e
+-- `strategy_approvals` guardam `client_id` e ficaram sem índice, enquanto as
+-- irmãs (`content_items`, `content_versions`, `strategies`,
+-- `strategy_versions`, `onboarding_submissions`) têm o seu. Isso importa agora
+-- porque a FASE 4 vai filtrar TODA leitura por `client_id` na policy: sem
+-- índice, cada consulta do portal vira varredura sequencial. Policy de RLS é
+-- caminho de leitura real — talvez o mais real de todos.
+create index content_comments_client_idx    on public.content_comments (client_id);
+create index content_approvals_client_idx   on public.content_approvals (client_id);
+create index strategy_approvals_client_idx  on public.strategy_approvals (client_id);
-- 
2.43.0


From a53731849e99e5582e2ad83bbd80fa00266f77e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:35:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/31] feat(db): generated types and an explicit enum parity
 contract
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`database.types.ts` comes straight from the schema. It is excluded from
Prettier and ESLint on purpose: fixing lint in generated code is work the
next regeneration undoes, and the header the generator now writes says so
where someone will actually read it.

`src/config/enums.ts` gains the eight Milestone 1 taxonomies it was
missing and, more usefully, a machine-checkable version of the ADR-0003
contract. `PG_ENUMS` maps each Postgres type name to its exact list of
values, in order — `enumsortorder` is what Postgres uses to compare and
sort, so a different order changes the result of `order by status`.

`PG_ENUMS_PENDENTES` is a deliberate tripwire. `file_category`,
`meeting_type` and `meeting_status` exist in TypeScript because the
prototype uses them, and do not exist in the database because their tables
arrive in phases 12 and 13. The tests require them to stay absent. The day
a migration creates one, the test breaks — and breaking is the right
behaviour: it is the reminder to move the key into `PG_ENUMS` in the same
PR instead of letting the two sources drift in silence.

