From 919ab9931992dbcf7f4691f4f4a26b4799ab301d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:35:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/31] test(db): 64 cases against a real Postgres
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Vitest splits into two projects. `unit` runs anywhere in seconds. `rls`
runs against a real database and never against a double — a mock of RLS
would test the mock (ADR-0015). When it finds no database it fails with
instructions rather than skipping: an isolation test that "passes" without
a database proves nothing.

The invariants run as `service_role` on purpose. It is the role that
ignores RLS, the most powerful one the system uses; if a rule holds there
it holds anywhere. That is the opposite of proving isolation with
`service_role`, which would be a mistake — isolation is RLS, and RLS is
phase 4. What is proved here is that triggers and constraints have no
exceptions.

The schema sweep does not know the tables by name; it asks the catalog.
That is what catches the table someone adds in a future migration without
enabling RLS. One case creates a throwaway table just to show that anon
and authenticated *would* be granted 14 privileges by default, so that
"zero privileges" reads as a result rather than a vacuous truth.

One case found a real design conflict rather than confirming an
assumption: `activity_log` rejects every UPDATE, and `actor_id` was
declared `on delete set null` — and SET NULL is an UPDATE. Deleting a
profile with any log row failed with a message about append-only that
explains nothing to whoever just wanted to remove a user. Fixed in the
migration, recorded in ADR-0019, and now covered by a pair: a person with
no trail is deleted through the cascade, a person with one is refused.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
