`docs/database.md` is new and is the operational manual: how to run with
and without Docker, what each migration brings, what the five `app`
functions guarantee, what the seed is for, how to compare two databases.
It opens by saying that RLS is enabled and has no policies, because that
is the sentence most likely to be misread as "the database is secure".

ADR-0019 records a decision the tests forced: the append-only log wins
over deleting a person. Whoever left a trail is disabled, not deleted —
which is the lifecycle the product already had. If erasure is ever
required, the path is anonymising the profile row, never rewriting the
log; the log stores identifiers and transitions, never content or PII, so
anonymising the profile already removes the personal data.

D-08 is settled and off the agenda: `boop_admin` global, `boop_member`
only with an explicit membership, `client_user` only its own tenant. The
seed materialises all three, including the member with no membership.

Two new entries in spec-review: I-12 for the append-only conflict above,
I-13 for the Milestone 1 ERD drawing `files` and `meetings` while the
roadmap builds them in phases 12 and 13. The roadmap wins — bringing
schema in ahead of the requirement creates tables nobody knows how to use.

R-15 (enum drift) moves from planned mitigation to implemented, in two
layers rather than one: one reads `pg_enum`, the other compares the
generated types without needing a database at all.

