Found while reading the framework docs bundled with the pinned version:
Next 16 deprecated `middleware.ts` and renamed it to `proxy.ts`, with the
exported function renamed from `middleware` to `proxy`. Every document
here still said `middleware.ts` — CLAUDE.md, security.md,
architecture.md, the roadmap and the security rules.

It is not only a name. The Proxy runs on the Node.js runtime by default
and rejects the `runtime` config option, so the Edge constraints that
used to shape library choices no longer apply. And the framework team
says plainly that the rename exists to discourage the feature: avoid it
unless nothing else works. That does not contradict our rule, it backs
it — this file refreshes a session and redirects, and never decides
authorization. R-08 reads better under the new name than the old one.

No file was created and no phase was started. Only the names were
corrected, so that phase 3 does not begin by writing a deprecated file,
and both halves of the pair moved together — the reasoning in `docs/`
and the obligation in `.claude/rules/`.

What stays open, recorded as I-14 and left for phase 3 to settle in an
ADR: whether that file should exist at all. `@supabase/ssr` has to
refresh the session somewhere and the documented Supabase pattern uses
exactly this hook, while Next now pushes the other way. The alternative
is refreshing inside each route, which spreads the responsibility and is
easy to forget on a new one. That trade-off belongs to the phase that
creates the file.

