From 7426ee8b71e06bb41c6a176add0c049752c462f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 01:43:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/31] =?UTF-8?q?feat(auth):=20phase=203=20=E2=80=94=20Mag?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ic=20Link,=20SSR=20session=20and=20a=20trustworthy=20Actor?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Boop OS now knows who is making the request, and it finds that out on
the server. Magic Link with PKCE, session in an httpOnly cookie, `proxy.ts`
refreshing the token, `getActor`/`requireActor`, protected routes, logout.

Two decisions the phase had to close, both as ADRs.

ADR-0020 keeps `proxy.ts`, with one responsibility. Next 16 renamed the file
to discourage it, and its own documentation explains why that matters here: a
Server Function is a POST to the route where it lives, so a matcher that
excludes a path also stops covering the actions there — a refactor can remove
proxy coverage with nobody noticing. A file that fragile refreshes a session;
it does not decide access. The guard that decides is `requireActor()` in the
render server, on the group layout, for every route in it.

ADR-0021 is temporary and dated. Migration 20260901140008 revoked every
privilege from `anon` and `authenticated`, and RLS is on with no policies, so
`getActor()` cannot read `profiles` with the user's JWT — not even by rpc,
since the `app` schema is revoked too. Identity is read with the service role,
server-side, always filtered by the id of the validated session, with a
minimal projection, and for identity alone. Three call sites, one table, plus
the activity log. The consequence is stated where it can be read rather than
buried: in this window the second layer of authorisation contributes nothing,
which is exactly why no screen was wired to real data.

The Actor carries identity and not scope. No `clientIds`: a membership read
through a client that ignores RLS would be the opposite of what that field is
supposed to prove. It arrives in phase 4, from the `app.*` functions.

Everything fails closed. No session, no profile row, `disabled`, `invited`, or
a failed read — each one is a redirect to /login with the right message, and a
missing profile is never turned into a new profile.

Two problems found while building, both worth naming:

  - The protected routes were being prerendered. `getActor()` checked the
    Supabase configuration before touching cookies, so the build resolved
    "nobody is logged in" and froze a redirect to /login — served to everyone,
    including people with a session. Reading the cookie first makes every
    route that asks who the user is dynamic by consequence, with nobody having
    to remember to mark it. There is a regression test that says so.

  - The post-login destination used to travel in the Magic Link URL, which
    would have forced a wildcard in the Supabase redirect list — and a
    wildcard accepts as a session destination any URL matching the pattern. It
    now travels in a short-lived httpOnly cookie, so the redirect list stays
    exact and the destination belongs to the browser that asked for the link
    rather than to whoever opens the email.

The login screen from phase 1.5 is untouched as a design; what changed is
underneath it. The prototype index at `/` gave way to a real decision: session
goes to the portal, no session goes to login, resolved server-side without a
flash of a screen the person was not allowed to see.

Email enumeration is closed by construction: the errors that would reveal
whether an account exists are silenced, and the confirmation is conditional —
"if this address has access". A login form that answers "not registered" is a
free consultant on who the Boop's clients are.

66 authentication tests, 219 in total. The client bundle was checked and
carries neither the service role nor the admin client.

