From dc386903eac96c9fa52c25dbb2a3765c1b0a85cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 02:54:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/31] docs: phase 3 validated against the hosted environment
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The full cycle ran on Vercel against boop-os-staging with a real inbox: link
requested, link opened, code exchanged for a session, profile promoted from
invited to active, user.joined written, protected route reached, logout, and
the protected route refused afterwards.

`authentication.md` gains the part that is worth keeping — how to tell, from
the database alone, that a login really happened. Three places in order: the
GoTrue audit trail, `last_sign_in_at`, and the activity log. It also records
the shape PKCE leaves behind, because that shape is a diagnosis: two `login`
entries a few hundred milliseconds apart are the verify step and the code
exchange, and a single one would mean some other flow was used.

No schema change, no policy, no migration. The security surface was checked
again after the run and is where phase 2 left it: RLS on all 19 tables, zero
policies, zero grants for anon and authenticated, `app` still revoked. The
activity log holds exactly one `user.joined` for a cycle that included a login
and a logout, which is the idempotence the promotion was written to have.

Neither the test address nor the auth user id is recorded here. A real inbox
in a committed document is the same mistake as a real client in the seed.

