From 5e5b59e102f91c15fc97b172835f1fb975591fd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 09:37:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/31] feat(auth): can(), scope guards, and service_role
 leaves the application
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ADR-0021 was accepted as temporary with a review due this phase. The review
happened, one point at a time, and none of the three kept service_role.
`grep -rn createSupabaseAdminClient src/` now returns only the file that
defines it.

getActor() reads `profiles` through the user's JWT, under RLS. One subtlety
stays deliberate: the "own profile" branch of app.has_profile_access() does not
require status = 'active'. If it did, getActor() would return null for both the
revoked and the not-yet-activated person, and the two would collapse into the
same generic redirect. The read grants exactly one row, and no other policy
derives access from it.

The other two could not become policies, for the same reason each: the
operation needs more privilege than its caller should hold permanently. So the
privilege was cut down to the size of the operation instead of left as root.
promote_invited_profile() takes no argument at all — there is nothing to point
at someone else — and does one transition. record_activity() derives actor_id
from auth.uid() rather than accepting it, because an audit log that can be
forged is worse than no audit log; client_id and project_id stay parameters and
are therefore checked against the caller's membership.

Promotion and the `user.joined` row now happen in one transaction. They used to
be two calls with a window where the promotion held and the event was lost.

can() is a pure table, one line per cell of docs/permissions.md, and it answers
by role only. Scope is not its job: a clientIds list assembled at the start of a
request is a photograph, so revoking a membership mid-request would have no
effect and the application would hold a second truth about access, competing
with RLS. requireClientAccess/requireProjectAccess ask by trying to read the
resource through the JWT — the same policy that guards the table answers the
guard, so the two can never disagree. Missing resource and other-tenant
resource return the identical 404, because a 403 confirms existence and that
confirmation is already information to someone cycling uuids.

The admin gate that phase 3 left inline became requireBoop(), so a new internal
route inherits it instead of having to remember it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_015WQ61xFhQmNQ876VDR35a3
