From 63e8889f171c38f2cb43855c2f8bf621b3cb0645 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 09:37:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/31] test: 267 cases against a real Postgres, written in
 pairs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Every case has both halves. A test that only checks the happy path proves
nothing about isolation: delete the seed row and it still passes.

isolation.test.ts declares a matrix of twelve tenant tables by six roles and
measures each cell against the row counts read as service_role, so "sees its
own tenant" means exactly the number of rows that exist and "does not see the
other" means zero. The `parcial` cells assert a strict inequality, so if the
internal redaction ever disappears the failure lands here too, not only in the
visibility file. The two columns that matter most are the flat ones: the
unlinked member and the disabled user are `nada` in every row.

adversarial.test.ts writes the SQL by hand, because that is the real scenario —
the Data API accepts whatever the browser assembles. Tenant spoofing on insert,
ownership reassignment on update, role escalation, membership self-grant,
forged approvals (attempted as boop_admin too), and audit rows written or
deleted directly. A denial counts either as a privilege error or as zero rows
affected; what may not happen is the write landing.

internal-visibility.test.ts covers the leak that is likelier than A reaching B:
the client seeing Boop's backstage inside their own account. It also asserts
two limitations rather than protections — clients.notes and internal_notes do
come down with the granted row, because RLS is row-level. Those two tests break
on the day that stops being true, which is the day docs/security.md has to
change with them.

policy-matrix.test.ts is the sweep: the declared matrix against the catalog,
policies equal to grants on all nineteen tables, no `using (true)`, every
UPDATE policy carrying both USING and WITH CHECK, every policy targeting
authenticated and deciding through an app.* function.

permissions.matrix.test.ts transcribes docs/permissions.md by hand instead of
importing policy.ts. Importing the source and checking it against itself would
only prove equality is reflexive; two independent writings of the same rule
fail when they drift.

schema.test.ts had two phase-2 assertions that this phase was expected to
invert — its own comment said so — plus the definer list, which grew.

