Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ADR-0021 was accepted temporary with a review due this phase. ADR-0022 is that
review, and it replaces rather than edits: the old text stays exactly as it
was, gaining only the superseded marker, because it describes the window
between phases 3 and 4 accurately and an accepted ADR is not rewritten.

It records the three service_role points separately, because they did not have
the same answer. getActor() moved to the JWT under RLS. The other two became
smaller named boundaries, and the reasoning is the part worth keeping: granting
UPDATE on `profiles` to authenticated would have granted role escalation along
with it, and granting INSERT on activity_log would have allowed a forged
actor_id. The answer was to shrink the privilege to the size of the operation,
not to keep root.

It also settles what ADR-0021 left open: clientIds does not join the Actor, and
that is now a decision rather than a pending item.

authorization.md is new and is the answer to "role x resource x operation"
without reading SQL — the nineteen-table matrix, the eleven functions, the
guards, and how to prove isolation locally. permissions.md was corrected where
it still treated actor.clientIds as the source of scope.

security.md gains two sections. One states plainly what RLS does not do: it is
row-level, so clients.notes and content_versions.internal_notes travel with the
granted row, and a column GRANT cannot separate the personas because
`authenticated` is a single role for all three. The protection is server-side
projection, nothing exposes them while the portal reads mocks, and phase 5 is
obliged to close it when it wires real data. The other classifies the Supabase
linter findings: zero RLS findings; the two security-definer warnings are the
design; leaked-password protection does not apply to a product with no
passwords; one unindexed foreign key became a migration and the rest did not,
with the reason for each.

