From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:36:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/31] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20phase=205=20=E2=80=94=20admin=20a?=
 =?UTF-8?q?nd=20clients=20on=20real=20data,=20with=20column-level=20debt?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20paid?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The administrative surface stops being a placeholder paragraph and becomes five
routes that read and write Supabase. No SQL by hand, no seed, no Studio, no
hardcoded data — which is the actual bar for this phase, not "Supabase returns a
client".

THE COLUMN-LEVEL DEBT, PAID

Phase 4 left this dated and due here: RLS is row-level, so clients.notes travels
on the row the policy grants, and a column GRANT cannot separate the three
personas because `authenticated` is one role for all of them.

The answer is three independent layers, and the middle one is the interesting
part. Projections are separated by AUDIENCE, not by a boolean: ClientPublic and
ClientDetail, never getClient({ includeNotes }). An argument has a default, can
be forgotten, can be inverted in a refactor, and leaves no trace in the type.
ClientPublic simply has no `notes` to leak.

AssertClientFacing<T> makes that a compile error rather than a review note. The
constraint is recursive and its failure arm is a NAMED object instead of `never`,
so the compiler says which field broke the rule:

  Type 'ClientPublic' does not satisfy the constraint
  '{ CAMPO_INTERNO_EM_PROJECAO_CLIENT_FACING: "notes" }'

content_versions.internal_notes is already in INTERNAL_FIELDS, before any content
query exists. Phase 10's first client-facing read is born under the same lock.

TWO NEW PRIVILEGED BOUNDARIES

ADR-0022 named this trigger exactly: "a people-administration operation (phase 5)
that needs to write profiles outside the promotion". Inviting sets a role and
disabling sets a status, and both live on the same row as `role`.

profiles keeps having no UPDATE policy and no UPDATE grant for anyone, admins
included. assign_invited_profile_role() and disable_profile() are security
definer, take the TARGET as a parameter and derive the CALLER from auth.uid() —
a parameter saying "about whom" is input; one saying "who am I" would be a blank
signature.

Three refusals live in the function bodies rather than in a policy, so each has an
adversarial test: boop_admin as a role value (the matrix has invite_client_user
and invite_boop_member and deliberately lacks the third line — boop_admin is
global under D-08, so a screen that mints one is the cheapest escalation in the
system); target equal to caller (self-disable has no way back in V0, and with a
single admin it is the door locked from the inside); and any profile past
`invited`.

SERVICE ROLE COMES BACK, ONCE, WHERE THE ADR SAID IT WOULD

Creating a row in auth.users. No application role writes there and no policy
fits — it is not a domain schema. The distinction that matters: phase 4 removed
service_role from AUTHORIZATION; this is Auth ADMINISTRATION.

createAdminClient() is no longer exported. What leaves admin.ts is
inviteAuthUser(), one named operation. Exporting the factory is how a bypass
comes back — someone instantiates it mid-workflow "just for this query".

THE INVITE NEEDED A SECOND DOOR IN THE CALLBACK

A server-initiated email link has no PKCE verifier: the invited person never
called signInWithOtp. With the default template GoTrue falls back to the implicit
flow and returns the session in the URL FRAGMENT, which never reaches the server —
the invite would die on `link_invalid`. The callback now also accepts
token_hash + type via verifyOtp, resolved entirely server-side. The `code` branch
is untouched. The Supabase template must point at it; documented as a manual step.

WHAT THE TESTS FOUND BEFORE QA WOULD HAVE

- z.email().trim() validated before trimming, so an address pasted with a space —
  the most common case there is — came back invalid.
- revokeClientAccess did not check what the DELETE removed. A DELETE policy
  FILTERS instead of refusing, so an unauthorised delete returns error = null and
  zero rows; the workflow would have written membership.revoked for a revocation
  that never happened. An audit log that lies is worse than one that is missing.
- Callout has role="status" and the aria-live region repeated the same sentence:
  screen readers said it twice.

474 tests to 622, all previous ones untouched. Two of the new suites are worth a
note: the RLS surface test imports the column constants from production code, so
what runs against Postgres is the string getClientPublic() passes to .select();
and the message test reads the mutations source and extracts every
WorkflowError('...'), because a transcribed list would go stale on the exact day
it needs to fail.

Staging fingerprint matches local across all nine parts. Zero RLS advisor
findings. What is NOT verified from here, and is item 1 of the manual checklist:
the PostgREST HTTP path — no Docker daemon for a local stack, and this
environment's network policy refuses CONNECT to the project.

