Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The hosted QA passed and left five events in the activity log, which is the
proof the PostgREST path this session could not reach works end to end. Checking
the invariants against that real data turned up a gap.

WHAT WAS OPEN

A policy decides which ROWS may be written. It says nothing about COLUMNS.
clients_update is `is_boop() and has_client_access(id)`, and that is all — anyone
past it rewrote the whole row. Reproduced on staging through the `authenticated`
role, carrying a real boop_admin identity:

  update public.clients set created_at = '2000-01-01' → 1 row
  update public.clients set created_by = null         → 1 row

The application never sends those columns: the schema is .strict() and the UPDATE
object names its fields. But the Data API is public, and the rule here is that the
application is not the last lock.

This is not tenant isolation and not privilege escalation — whoever can do it
already reached the row. It is audit integrity: forging who created an account and
when. In a system whose whole value is recording decisions, a created_by that
accepts being rewritten is worth less than none.

The sweep found the same shape in 12 tables. All of them have carried the UPDATE
grant since phase 4; they are empty, not protected. Fixing one of twelve instances
of one finding would be leaving eleven holes open on purpose, so the migration
covers all of them. No feature, query or workflow is added — it is defensive DDL
over tables that already exist.

TWO RULES, BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO LEGITIMATE BEHAVIOURS

The first version made created_by strictly immutable, and phase 2's suite caught
it: `quem deixou rastro no log não pode ser apagado` started failing with 23514
instead of the 23503 it asserts.

The authorship FKs are `on delete set null` on purpose — deleting a person must
not destroy the client they created, so the schema WANTS that column to go null at
that moment (ADR-0019). A strict trigger made it impossible to delete anyone who
had ever created anything.

So the rule is more precise than "immutable": authorship may be CLEARED, never
REASSIGNED. `someone → null` is the cascade; `someone → someone else` and
`null → someone` are forgery, and those are what to stop. created_at stays strict.

onboarding_submissions.submitted_by is deliberately excluded: it starts null and
is filled by the UPDATE that submits the onboarding (phase 7). Authorship settled
mid-life is not authorship of creation.

The regression suite includes a sweep rather than a transcribed list, because a
list needs remembering when phase 6 adds the next table, and nobody remembers.

WHAT THE QA DID NOT EXERCISE, recorded rather than papered over: there is no
client.updated in the log and updated_at equals created_at, so editing a client
was never performed — the trigger works, verified in a rolled-back transaction, so
the gap is the step, not the mechanism. And the invited person's last_seen_at is
null: the email went out, nobody came in through it.

622 to 628 tests. Staging fingerprint matches local across all nine parts again.
Zero RLS advisor findings.

